    "title": "Federal Judge Finds ICE Arrests in Colorado 'Unlawful'",
    "text": "• U.S. District Judge R. Brooke Jackson ruled that ICE officers acted unlawfully in their arrests of suspected illegal immigrants in Colorado.\n• The ruling, a 66-page order, was issued on Tuesday.\n• The judge sided with four individuals found to be illegally present in the U.S. who had 'deep and longstanding ties to their communities'.\n• The judge found that ICE made warrantless arrests without probable cause of flight risk.\n• The four immigrants were represented by the American Civil Liberties Union (ACLU).\n• The lawsuit argued ICE agents were 'indiscriminately stopping and arresting people' based on their skin color.\n• The judge found that ICE could not make warrantless arrests and then hold individuals without probable cause and evidence of flight risk.\n• The judge found that the plaintiffs were likely harmed by ICE's actions, spending between two weeks and three months in custody.\n• The judge issued a preliminary injunction to stop the arrests without probable cause.\n• ICE has almost doubled its headcount in Colorado this year and plans to open three additional detention centers.\n• The judge found that the plaintiffs can represent a class of all individuals arrested since January 20, without a warrant and without a pre-arrest assessment of flight risk.\n• The administration has been ordered to repay the plaintiffs for their posted bonds.\n• The ACLU of Colorado celebrated the decision, stating that ICE cannot use haphazard warrantless arrests.\n• The Department of Homeland Security (DHS) indicated it would appeal the ruling.\n• DHS stated the ruling was an effort to prevent the Trump administration from deporting criminal illegal aliens.",
